<spell-notebook "keys usage">
	<header level="1">
		Keys
		<header>Keys show a keyboard shortcut as key caps, the way the reader's own keyboard writes it.</>
	</>
	<keys-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-keys>` draws a keyboard shortcut as key caps, the way the reader's own keyboard writes it.

			- `shortcut` is the keys, joined by `+`:  `"mod+S"`, `"shift+mod+P"`, `"escape"`.
			- `mod` (or `modifier`) is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere:  write it for the app's main shortcut key.
			- `ctrl`, `alt` (Option on a Mac), `shift` and `meta` are those keys themselves.
			- Two shortcuts, a space apart, draw both.
			- `platform` draws a Mac's keys (`apple`) or everyone else's (`other`), whatever the reader has.

			It only draws the shortcut.  Making the keys do something is the page's:  `UI.keyboard` reads the same words.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			Screen readers hear words, not symbols:  `<ui-keys shortcut="mod+S">` is read as "Command S" on a Mac,
			"Control S" elsewhere.  The words are translated with the page's language.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
